<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.9.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01012</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.9.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fb15e85d-2b4c-40b5-9119-d3771a45a551</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="675acbd6-5e73-441a-be8e-e0afa668c7d4" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="fb4582f9-6add-436e-a74f-586af09e3a74" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Car A" data-solution-options="[&quot;Car A&quot;, &quot;Car B&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The graphs show the distance, \(d\), traveled by two cars, A and B, over time, \(t\). Distance is measured in miles, and time is measured in hours. Which car traveled slower? <figure><media alt="Lines A and B on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d71d8dc6f83c6c24781699b463b8e5790cc17f77"/></media></figure></para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Car A traveled slower because Car B was a farther distance in the same amount of time as Car A.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="43d7eb08-a3ef-4608-a848-3f943fd46fcc" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Graph 3" data-solution-options="[&quot;Graph 1&quot;, &quot;Graph 2&quot;, &quot;Graph 3&quot;, &quot;Graph 4&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For questions 2 – 5, use the graphs below to match each description of a situation in which the volume of water in a tank is a function of time to its corresponding graph. </para>
      <div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
        <div class="os-raise-mx-auto">
          <para>Graph 1<figure><media alt="Graph on coordinate plane, no grid, Origin O. V shaped graph, line begins on Y axis, Vertex points down."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6cc1b51e52fcacb2ed1d0724e6690e6cf5b54917"/></media></figure></para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-mx-auto">
          <para>Graph 2<figure><media alt="Graph on coordinate plane, no grid, Origin O. Line begins at origin, moves upwards and to right. Steeper second segment."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/357be93d48a907c00f0d9b6d419adc447302cf1c"/></media></figure></para>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
        <div class="os-raise-mx-auto">
          <para>Graph 3<figure><media alt="Graph on coordinate plane, no grid, Origin O. V shaped graph, line begins on at origin, Vertex points up."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c77becae591a5010e70ef56d22d0e13b4b6dd002"/></media></figure></para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-mx-auto">
          <para>Graph 4<figure><media alt="Graph on coordinate plane, no grid, Origin O. Line begins at origin, moves upwards and to right, decreases briefly, then increases again."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/319837f6ca9d40f80bd22a7dae92190ef6ef2b3e"/></media></figure></para>
        </div>
      </div>
      <para>An empty 20-gallon water tank is filled at a constant rate for 3 minutes until it is half full. Then, it is emptied at a constant rate for 3 minutes.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Graph 3</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="04a914d5-1a20-4a61-8d16-2929c8a4328f" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Graph 1" data-solution-options="[&quot;Graph 1&quot;, &quot;Graph 2&quot;, &quot;Graph 3&quot;, &quot;Graph 4&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A full 10-gallon water tank is drained for 30 seconds, until it is half full. Afterward, it gets refilled.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Graph 1</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1fb4f2f9-3fd6-4178-9685-d6fece3b0815" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Graph 2" data-solution-options="[&quot;Graph 1&quot;, &quot;Graph 2&quot;, &quot;Graph 3&quot;, &quot;Graph 4&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A 2,000-gallon water tank starts out empty. It is being filled for 5 hours, slowly at first, and faster later.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Graph 2</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="594de829-a3ec-448e-8b1c-b95c36a43989" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Graph 4" data-solution-options="[&quot;Graph 1&quot;, &quot;Graph 2&quot;, &quot;Graph 3&quot;, &quot;Graph 4&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>An empty 100-gallon water tank is filled in 50 minutes. Then, a dog jumps in and splashes around for 10 minutes, letting 7 gallons of water out. The tank is refilled afterward.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Graph 4</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>
<para>For questions 6 – 7, use this situation and function:</para>

<blockquote>
  The daily cost to the manufacturing company is modeled by the function \(C(x)=7.25x+2500\), where \(C(x)\) is the total daily cost and \(x\) is the number of items manufactured. 
</blockquote>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="64daad2b-01c5-49bf-a1eb-734f596ded2d" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f14d1917-2b7c-42b3-9389-2b0ff44689fc" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2500">
    <problem>
      <para> What value equals \(C(0)\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 2500</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="fa3eeebd-3640-4077-bc4e-fd233737d66e" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Cost before any items are manufactured" data-solution-options="[&quot;When the cost is 0&quot;, &quot;Horizontal intercept&quot;, &quot;Cost before any items are manufactured&quot;, &quot;Maximum cost&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What does \(C(0)\) mean in the context of the situation?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Cost before any items are manufactured</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.9.1 – 4.9.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div>
